  "body": {
    "id": "thm:anchor-agreement-and-boundary-coverage",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "At the selected-law factorization level, \\(\\mathfrak M\\) is the finite four-response-type specialization of Condition 1 of Shingaki--Yoshida--Kuroki (2026). The design-A fiber \\(\\mathcal F_A(p,m)\\) is the subfiber obtained by fixing the target-population cell margins \\(P(E)=m\\). The design-B fiber \\(\\mathcal F_B(p)\\) satisfies their equation (13), together with instrument relevance. Consequently, on every compatible design-A law satisfying their regular Conditions 2--3, Theorem 1 transfers by restriction and the value returned by \\(\\mathscr A\\) equals their PN/PS value from Appendix equations (A.28)--(A.33). On every compatible regular design-B law satisfying Conditions 2--3, Corollary 1 transfers directly and \\(\\mathscr A\\) returns the same PN/PS value.\n\nThe additional contribution is classification on compatible singular and boundary observable laws, including design-A rank loss, coincident endpoints, inactive rows, and forced-zero incident coefficients and the analogous design-B response-level shared-\\(Z\\) residual-forest strata. On every such compatible law the atlas returns either an exact target-rigidity certificate or legal target-changing full-SCM twins; an empty fiber is reported separately as incompatibility. This is a strict compatible singular/boundary classification extension, not an extension of the published regular formula and not a blanket subsumption claim.",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:response-consistency",
      "ass:outcome-dependent-selection",
      "ass:proxy-exclusion",
      "ass:iv-exogeneity",
      "ass:iv-relevance",
      "def:anchor-model",
      "def:two-proxy-fiber",
      "def:iv-fiber",
      "lem:shingaki-yoshida-kuroki-regular-identification",
      "lem:shingaki-yoshida-kuroki-regular-iv-identification",
      "thm:mixed-support-elimination-complete",
      "thm:full-rigidity-atlas"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem states the exact published transfer: Theorem 1 by fixed-margin restriction in design A and Corollary 1 through equation (13) in design B.",
    "gap": "The published regular formulas do not classify compatible singular and boundary fibers or incompatible inputs.",
    "consumer": "Users can separate regular formula agreement from the new singular-boundary classification without a subsumption claim.",
    "proof_tex": "Condition 1 of Shingaki--Yoshida--Kuroki has the selected-law factorization\n\\[\n p(x,y,s,w,z,u)\n =p(s\\mid x,y)p(y\\mid x,u)p(x\\mid z,u)\n   p(w\\mid u)p(z\\mid u)p(u). \\tag{6}\n\\]\nResponse consistency makes \\(p(y\\mid x,u)\\) the deterministic kernel encoded by the four response types.  Outcome-dependent selection supplies \\(p(s\\mid x,y)\\), proxy exclusion supplies \\(p(w\\mid u)\\), and the treatment, second-proxy, and response-type mechanisms in \\(P_{\\vartheta}\\) supply the remaining factors.  Hence, at the selected-law factorization level, \\(\\mathfrak M\\) is the finite four-response-type specialization of that condition.\n\nThe design-A definition adds\n\\[\n P_{\\vartheta}(E,W,Z\\mid S=1)=p,\n \\qquad P_{\\vartheta}(E)=m. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_A(p,m)\\) is the subfiber obtained by fixing the population cell margins.  On a compatible member satisfying the published regular Conditions 2--3, the cited regular-identification result applies pointwise.  If \\(R_A(p)\\) denotes the pair computed by Appendix equations (A.28)--(A.33), that result gives\n\\[\n \\tau(\\vartheta)=R_A(p)\n \\quad\\text{for every }\\vartheta\\in\\mathcal F_A(p,m). \\tag{8}\n\\]\nCompatibility makes the fiber nonempty, and therefore\n\\[\n \\mathcal T_A(p,m)=\\{R_A(p)\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThe full-rigidity theorem returns the unique element of this target set.  This is transfer by restriction: fixing \\(m\\) cannot alter a conclusion valid for every member of the larger regular fiber.\n\nFor design B, instrument exogeneity changes the last two factors in (6) to\n\\(p(z\\mid u)p(u)=g(z)p(u)\\).  Every member consequently obeys\n\\[\n p(x,y,s,w,z,u)\n =p(s\\mid x,y)p(y\\mid x,u)p(x\\mid z,u)\n   p(w\\mid u)g(z)p(u), \\tag{10}\n\\]\nwhich is equation (13) of the cited paper.  The definition of \\(\\mathcal F_B(p)\\) additionally imposes instrument relevance.  On a compatible law satisfying Conditions 2--3, the cited regular IV identification result applies directly to (10).  Writing its observable answer as \\(R_B(p)\\),\n\\[\n \\tau(\\vartheta)=R_B(p)\n \\quad\\text{for every }\\vartheta\\in\\mathcal F_B(p),\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal T_B(p)=\\{R_B(p)\\}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThe full-rigidity theorem again returns this unique value.\n\nEquations (8) and (11) have only the published regular scope.  Outside it, the mixed-support theorem exhausts rank-two orientations, rank-one \\(\\mathrm L,\\mathrm R,\\mathrm E,\\mathrm D\\) modes, all coefficient-support faces, shared endpoints, and, in design B, every residual-forest and active-cycle state.  The full-rigidity theorem then reports incompatibility for an empty full fiber and, on every compatible singular or boundary law, returns either the exact common target or legal target-changing SCM twins.  This is a classification extension on compatible singular and boundary fibers; it neither extends the published regular formula beyond its hypotheses nor asserts blanket subsumption.",
